{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 47, :comments ["<b>And you shall take five, etc.</b> One needs to know why it says ״וְלָקַחְתָּ״ with a vav, since this statement is the decree of the statement <b>and those to be redeemed of the three hundred</b>, etc.? Perhaps it is because by this He drew attention to the intent of the preceding verse, ״and those to be redeemed of the three hundred,״ etc., whose decree is ״from the firstborn of the children of Israel״ — meaning that he should take the redemption of the firstborn who exceed the Levites from the firstborn and not from the Levites, for you might have thought to say that just as they redeemed the Levites' animals with their animals, so too they would redeem the remainder from them, and it would be a fortiori. Therefore Scripture says ״from the firstborn of the children of Israel״ — meaning, the redemption shall be from them. And so it says in the narrative, <b>and Moses took</b>, etc., <b>from the firstborn of the children of Israel he took the silver</b>. And He added to state a second commandment, that the redemption should be five shekels, and that is His saying ״וְלָקְחַתָּ״ with the addition of a vav. And He returned to say <b>you shall take</b>, that he should weigh the shekel by the sacred shekel which is doubled, and that this thing is indispensable for the redemption."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:06:01.047663619Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}